    "narrative": "Today\u2019s offensive outlook for the Zurich Zebras centers on two high-upside plays against veteran right-handers. **Junior Caminero** (76.17) draws the most favorable environment, as his matchup against Max Scherzer\u2014who carries an elevated 5.08 xERA\u2014combined with a strong #2 spot in the order, makes him our top-scoring option. Close behind is **Yordan Alvarez** (78.05), who remains locked in; his elite xwOBA metrics and favorable platoon split against Walbert Ure\u00f1a make him a cornerstone for production today. We are also leaning heavily on our speed threats, specifically **Trea Turner** (73.73) and **Caleb Durbin** (63.15). While **Trea Turner** faces the formidable Sandy Alcantara, his historical BvP edge (+3%) and the efficiency of the #2 spot mitigate the SP skill risk, while **Caleb Durbin** is a priority start specifically for his stolen base potential against the relatively slow delivery of Brandon Pfaadt.\n\nThe bench decision for this slate was razor-thin, with **Chase Meidroth** (44.84) serving as the final player cut. While **Chase Meidroth** comfortably sits above the 40-point floor, the optimizer prioritized the specific platoon advantages of **Christian Yelich**\u2014whose dynamic platoon split against Logan Gilbert provides a +13% boost\u2014and the leadoff utility of **Kyle Schwarber** (70.51). **Kazuma Okamoto** remains in the UTIL slot despite a modest 37.73 score, hovering just below our threshold but retained due to a lack of bench depth for this specific rotation. We are operating with caution regarding **Colt Keith** (45.44) and **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (53.28); both are facing elite opposing arms in Skenes and Sean Newcomb, respectively. **Pete Crow-Armstrong** specifically faces a daunting -15% drag from his platoon split, and while he is currently active, he serves as our primary candidate for mid-game substitution should his early-at-bat performance struggle against the lefty.",
